          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Late Recognition</strong><br>\nIt was only in the later years of his life that Bruckner's music began to receive broader appreciation. His symphonies started to be performed more frequently, and he was honored by musical societies and academic institutions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nBruckner died on October 11, 1896, in Vienna. After his death, his music gained widespread acceptance and today is regarded as a critical link between classical and romantic music. His symphonies, in particular, have influenced a wide range of composers and are celebrated for their originality and profound emotional depth.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Remembrance</strong><br>\nToday, Anton Bruckner is remembered for his unique contributions to music, especially the symphony. Concert halls around the world regularly perform his works, ensuring that his legacy continues to inspire and move audiences.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
